examples.solution_text:
Both functions have domain $\mathbb R$, so their shared domain is $\mathbb R$.
$\begin{aligned}f(x)+g(x)&=x^2-4x+6\\&=(x-2)^2+2\end{aligned}$
Since $(x-2)^2\ge0$, the minimum output is $2$, attained at $x=2$.
$D=\mathbb R,\quad R=[2,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.45cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-1,0)--(5.3,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,11) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1,2,3,4,5}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10}{\draw[black] (-0.08,\y)--(0.08,\y);\path (0,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=-0.8:4.8,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x-2)*(\x-2)+2});
\path (4.8,7) node[curveBlue,right] {$f(x)+g(x)$};
\fill[black] (2,2) circle (1.4pt);
\path (2,2) node[below right] {$(2,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.question_text:
Let $f(x)=x^2-6x$ and $g(x)=13$. The graph shows $y=f(x)+g(x)$.
Use the graph to find the domain and range of $f+g$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.4cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,14) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10,12}{\draw[black] (-0.08,\y)--(0.08,\y);\path (0,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=0:6,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x-3)*(\x-3)+4});
\path (4.8,10.5) node[curveBlue,right] {$f(x)+g(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The arrows show that the graph continues in both horizontal directions, so the domain is $\mathbb R$.
The lowest point is $(3,4)$ and lies on the graph. The outputs extend upwards without bound.
$D=\mathbb R,\quad R=[4,\infty)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.75cm,y=0.4cm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-0.5,0)--(6.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.5)--(0,14) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {1,2,3,4,5,6}{\draw[black] (\x,-0.12)--(\x,0.12);\path (\x,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {2,4,6,8,10,12}{\draw[black] (-0.08,\y)--(0.08,\y);\path (0,\y) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\y$};}
\draw[curveBlue,thick,<->,domain=0:6,samples=81] plot (\x,{(\x-3)*(\x-3)+4});
\path (4.8,10.5) node[curveBlue,right] {$f(x)+g(x)$};
\draw[black,dashed] (0,4)--(3,4)--(3,0);
\fill[black] (3,4) circle (1.4pt);
\path (3,4) node[below right] {$(3,4)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
